<h2>The default machinery stops at parents</h2>
<p>Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — the successor to Section 125 CrPC — is deliberately narrow about who owes whom. It binds a person of sufficient means to maintain the spouse, the minor child (and, in defined situations, an adult child unable to maintain itself), and his or her father or mother. The grandparent&ndash;grandchild relationship appears nowhere in it, in either direction. The summary, magistrate-level remedy therefore cannot be aimed at a grandfather while the real defaulter is a living, able-bodied parent — and courts are rightly resistant to attempts to convert the provision into a family-wide liability net.</p>

<h2>Route one: grandchildren as dependants under HAMA</h2>
<p>The Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956 operates on a different premise: not neglect by a living parent, but the death of the family&rsquo;s earning member. Section 21 defines the &ldquo;dependants&rdquo; of a deceased Hindu — a list that includes the widow, parents, minor sons, unmarried daughters, and, critically for the present question, the minor grandson and the unmarried granddaughter of a predeceased son, where they cannot obtain maintenance from their own parents or from their parents&rsquo; estate. Section 22 then supplies the obligation: the heirs of the deceased who take his estate must maintain the dependants out of that estate, each in proportion to the share taken.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The trigger is a death.</strong> The dependant claim arises against the estate of a deceased Hindu — typically the grandfather — not against a living grandparent&rsquo;s personal income.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The claimant must be a listed dependant.</strong> Grandchildren qualify through a predeceased parent, and only while minor (grandsons) or unmarried (granddaughters), and only to the extent their own parents&rsquo; resources cannot provide.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The liability follows the estate.</strong> Heirs who took nothing owe nothing; heirs who took shares owe proportionately, and a dependant who inherited a share is to that extent outside the claim.</div>
</div>
<p>In practice this route matters most for widowed daughters-in-law and their children living in the late grandfather&rsquo;s property, when the extended family controls the estate: the claim is framed as a civil suit for maintenance against the heirs, with the estate as the measure.</p>

<h2>Route two: the Senior Citizens Act runs the other way</h2>
<p>The Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007 widens the net in the opposite direction. Its definition of &ldquo;children&rdquo; includes son, daughter, grandson and grand-daughter — excluding minors — and it entitles a parent or grandparent unable to maintain himself from his own earnings or property to apply to the Maintenance Tribunal for a monthly allowance. The obligation rests on the relative&rsquo;s capacity and the senior citizen&rsquo;s need, the procedure is summary, and the forum is a tribunal rather than a civil court. For elderly people whose own children have died or are themselves without means, adult grandchildren are squarely within reach.</p>

<h2>The map, side by side</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Statute</th><th>Forum</th><th>Key condition</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Minor child against parent</td><td>Section 144 BNSS / Section 20 HAMA</td><td>Magistrate / Family Court; civil court</td><td>Parent&rsquo;s means and neglect</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grandchild against grandfather&rsquo;s estate</td><td>Sections 21&ndash;22 HAMA</td><td>Civil court / Family Court</td><td>Grandfather deceased; parent predeceased; claimant unable to obtain maintenance from parents&rsquo; estate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grandparent against adult grandchild</td><td>Senior Citizens Act, 2007</td><td>Maintenance Tribunal</td><td>Senior citizen unable to maintain himself; grandchild not a minor</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Choose the statute before drafting: a dependant claim misfiled as a Section 144 BNSS petition against grandparents invites dismissal on maintainability, costing months.</p>
  <p>In dependant claims, plead and prove the estate — what the deceased left, who took it, and in what shares — because the estate is both the basis and the ceiling of the liability.</p>
  <p>For senior citizens, the 2007 Act&rsquo;s tribunal route is faster than a civil suit and sits alongside, not in place of, rights under HAMA; the appropriate forum depends on what is really sought — a monthly allowance, or protection of residence and property.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states the general statutory position for information. Cross-generational maintenance claims are sensitive to personal law, to the estate&rsquo;s composition and to each family&rsquo;s facts, and specific advice should be taken before filing.</div>
